From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] tcpmon, localspl, winspool: printers on the network through Windows drivers; language monitors; the printer's state

A printer with a Windows driver could print only to a local port (or a
CUPS queue). Now, as on Windows:

- tcpmon: Windows' Standard TCP/IP Port monitor, from its documented Xcv
  interface (tcpxcv.h): AddPort, ConfigPort, DeletePort and GetConfigInfo
  with PORT_DATA_1, the ports kept where Windows keeps them (Monitors\
  Standard TCP/IP Port\Ports) so the spooler finds their monitor, and
  listed by EnumPorts. A job goes raw on a TCP port (9100) or by LPR
  (RFC 1179, into the port's queue); a port named by its URL (ipp://,
  ipps://, http://, https://) sends the job by IPP Print-Job (RFC 8011).
  Makers' installers and our Settings make such ports.
- localspl: a job's port goes through its driver's language monitor
  (OpenPortEx with the port monitor), as Windows' spooler does; the port is
  told the job's printer and job id (a TODO). A maker's monitor that fails
  or crashes is left out and the job goes to the port itself.
- winspool: what a language monitor says of the port (SetPort,
  PORT_INFO_3: toner low, paper out, offline...) is the printer's status
  in GetPrinter.

Zebra's language monitor now runs on a network port (and falls back when
it fails starting a job).

Test: test/netport-gate.sh with test/netport-server.py,
test/netport-probe.c and test/langmon-monitor.c, our own (mutants
SG_MUTANT_RAWPORT, LPR, IPP, XCVADDPORT, LANGMON, PORTSTATUS).
